StoneFossil in-process serving patch (Fossil 2.26)
==================================================

Fossil is designed as one process per HTTP request: it serves a request and
then exit()s, discarding all process state. Stone embeds the Fossil core in a
single long-lived process (the in-process loopback server in
ios/FossilBridge/StoneFossil.c) and calls fossil_main() once per request. That
re-entry is mostly safe because fossil_main() begins with memset(&g,0,...), but
several modules keep *request-scoped* state in file-scope statics that Fossil
otherwise relies on the process exit to clear. Reused across requests, that
stale state causes redirect loops, dangling-pointer crashes and assert()
failures.

This patch adds a small `*_reset_state()` to each affected module and calls them
all from the single HTTP request entry point, cgi_handle_http_request():

  src/cgi.c      cgi_reset_state()    reply status/headers/content, query params
  src/etag.c     etag_reset_state()   the once-per-request ETag buffer
  src/cookies.c  cookie_reset_state() display-prefs cookie cache (dangling ptrs)
  src/style.c    style_reset_state()  submenu buttons/controls, header flags, JS

It also guards one piece of *process-scoped* one-time init that the reset model
does not cover:

  src/main.c     a once-flag around the two sqlite3_config() calls. Those are
                 only valid before SQLite initializes; on the second and later
                 fossil_main() calls they return SQLITE_MISUSE(21). Made
                 idempotent so they run exactly once per process.

A third category, added for ticket 3a4b6c6bb8 (SIGABRT in
manifest_crosslink_begin on the phone's second sync of a session): statics
that are scoped to *one fossil_main() invocation*, not one HTTP request, so
they need resetting at the top of fossil_main() itself -- src/main.c, right
after the existing memset(&g,...) -- rather than at the HTTP entry point,
since plain CLI-style commands (sync, clone, configuration pull) never go
through cgi_handle_http_request() at all:

  src/manifest.c manifest_reset_state()  three statics client_sync's manifest
                 crosslinking touches, all of which Fossil's own comments
                 already describe as living for "this session" / needing an
                 atexit() handler to clear -- upstream assumes the process
                 exits after one invocation, which the embedded server never
                 does:
                   manifest_crosslink_busy            asserted ==0 on entry;
                     a fossil_fatal() mid-crosslink (any error thrown while
                     applying pulled configuration, e.g. a permission the
                     login lacks) skips manifest_crosslink_end() and leaves
                     it set, so the *next* sync's manifest_crosslink_begin()
                     trips the assert and SIGABRTs the whole process.
                   seenManifests                       a has-seen Bag whose
                     own doc comment says it's freed "only from the app's
                     atexit() handler" -- persists past db_close() even
                     though the rows it caches state about were served by a
                     since-closed connection.
                   manifest_event_triggers_are_enabled guards recreating a
                     `temp.alert_trigger1` TEMP TRIGGER, which db_close()
                     drops at the end of *every* invocation (success or
                     failure) -- the flag surviving means the trigger is
                     silently never recreated after the first call in the
                     process, on every subsequent invocation, not just an
                     aborted one.

  src/db.c       db_reset_delete_on_fail()  found while writing the Linux
                 reproduction (scripts/linux-crosslink-test-driver.c), not
                 named in the original crash trace, but the same class of
                 bug and worse in effect: db_delete_on_failure() registers
                 a just-created file (a fresh "init"/"clone" destination)
                 to be deleted if the current command fails, and nothing
                 ever un-registers it on success. A stale registration from
                 an earlier, already-successful invocation survives into a
                 later, unrelated one; the next db_force_rollback() for ANY
                 reason deletes that earlier file. Confirmed by reproducing
                 it: the test driver's own freshly-initialized repo was
                 silently deleted by an unrelated later db_force_rollback()
                 call before this fix.

Scope is deliberately minimal: only request- and invocation-scoped statics are
reset, and only the SQLite global-config init is made idempotent. Other
process-scoped one-time initialization (SQL function registration, table
loads, etc.) is intentionally left untouched. Regenerate the patch if the
pinned Fossil version changes; gen-fossil-sources.sh applies it and fails
loudly if it no longer applies cleanly.

